  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">WETLANDS RESTORATION AS WINTERING HABITA</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Tamaulipas coastal plains adjacent to the Laguna Madre, one of the world&apos;s largest coastal lagoons and an area of great importance for migratory birds, have experienced a significant expansion of agriculture, which has altered the freshwater wetlands. These wetlands serve as refuge to waterfowl, a source for drinking water, and removes the salt that waterfowl ingest while feeding in the seagrass beds of the hypersaline waters of the Laguna Madre. This project will restore 2,781 acres of freshwater wetlands as waterfowl and shorebird habitat. It will also protect 5,485.87 acres, including the restored area, through a ten-year conservation agreement.</narrative>
  </description>
